Probe of the F6 (cairn-m1-cqt.5.6) P-6d precondition, 2026-09-08, on
leanprover/lean4:v4.34.0-rc1, in a mathlib-free scratch lake project holding one
lean_lib `Solution` with glob `Solution.+`. Commands run through cairn.lean, so the
toolchain resolution is the gate's own.

Q1. Is `import Lean` admissible in a Solution-shaped module?

  lake build Solution   rc 0   Built Solution.Forge (10s)
  #check @Environment.addDeclCore
  @Environment.addDeclCore : Environment ->
    USize -> USize -> Declaration -> Option IO.CancelToken -> optParam Bool true ->
    Except Kernel.Exception Environment

Q2. Does an unchecked THEOREM survive the build and fail kernel replay?
    Fixture: forge_unchecked_theorem.lean, Declaration.thmDecl named `forged` with
    type `False` and value `trivial`, added with doCheck := false.

  lake build Solution                        rc 0   Built Solution.Forge (7.7s)
  lake env leanchecker -v Solution.Forge     rc 1
      leanchecker found a problem in Solution.Forge
      uncaught exception: while replaying declaration 'forged':
      (kernel) declaration type mismatch, 'forged' has type
        True
      but it is expected to have type
        False
  lake env leanchecker --fresh -v Solution.Forge   rc 1   same kernel exception

Q3. Does an unchecked AXIOM behave the same?
    Fixture: forge_unchecked_axiom.lean, Declaration.axiomDecl named `forged` with
    type `False`, added with doCheck := false, then `theorem forged_false : False`.

  lake build Solution                              rc 0   Built Solution.Forge (19s)
  lake env leanchecker -v Solution.Forge           rc 0   replaying Solution.Forge
  lake env leanchecker --fresh -v Solution.Forge   rc 0   replaying Solution.Forge with --fresh

WHAT Q3 MEANS FOR THE ORDERED PLAN. The kernel replay does not catch a forged axiom,
and it is right not to: an axiom carries no proof for the kernel to disagree with, so
replaying it is a no-op. The axiom computation is the only step that sees it, and the
kernel replay is the only step that sees the unchecked theorem. Neither step subsumes
the other and a plan carrying one of them is open to the forgery the other catches.
The two planted negatives therefore stop at different steps by construction, which is
the property F6's plan record has to make visible rather than assert.

NO-CLAIM. These fixtures were built in a mathlib-free scratch project, not under the
Challenge protocol, and no Solution build path exists in src/cairn/ yet, so this
establishes what the toolchain does and not what the gate does. Whether the protocol
should admit `import Lean` in a Solution is a separate decision from whether Lean
admits it; this probe answers only the second. The 12s and 19s figures are single
observations on a loaded machine.

Q4. What does each kernel replay variant cost on a module importing Lean?
    Same forge_unchecked_theorem.lean fixture, a fresh mathlib-free scratch project,
    both commands built from bundle/lean.json through cairn.lean.

  lake build Forge                              rc 0   8370 ms
  lake env leanchecker -v Forge                 rc 1   3711 ms
  lake env leanchecker --fresh -v Forge         rc 1   302343 ms

  Both variants refuse with the same kernel exception ("declaration type mismatch,
  'forged' has type True but it is expected to have type False"). --fresh replays every
  import, so on a module importing Lean it replays Lean core: 302 s, against a 180 s
  per-test cap and a 1080 s CI session deadline. The trusting variant replays the module
  alone. Single observations on one machine.
